WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579
Read tools and write tools.

2
00:00:01.859 --> 00:00:04.205
Sort every tool into one of two buckets.

3
00:00:04.485 --> 00:00:08.816
Read tools observe: look up a teammate, resolve a date, search a ticket queue.

4
00:00:09.096 --> 00:00:15.155
They are safe to retry, safe to run in parallel, and their worst failure is a wrong answer you can check.

5
00:00:15.435 --> 00:00:20.704
Write tools change something: create a task, send a message, update a record, spend money.

6
00:00:20.984 --> 00:00:23.885
Their failures persist after the conversation ends.

7
00:00:24.165 --> 00:00:38.032
The parallel tool use docs make the execution consequence explicit: independent read-only calls are usually safe to run concurrently, while tools with side effects or ordering requirements may be better run in sequence.

8
00:00:38.312 --> 00:00:45.523
Write tools also need to be idempotent, meaning that running the same call twice has the same effect as running it once.

9
00:00:45.803 --> 00:00:47.744
Agent loops make repeats likely.

10
00:00:48.024 --> 00:00:50.221
A request times out and is retried.

11
00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:52.699
The model loses track and asks again.

12
00:00:52.979 --> 00:01:05.693
Stripe’s API documentation shows the standard pattern: the client sends an idempotency key, and the server returns the saved result for a repeated key instead of performing the operation twice.

13
00:01:05.973 --> 00:01:12.160
For create_task, a key derived from owner, title, and due date, checked before you append, does the job.

14
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.872
Then grade write tools by reversibility.

15
00:01:15.152 --> 00:01:18.459
Creating a draft task in your own tracker is cheap to undo.

16
00:01:18.739 --> 00:01:24.029
Posting to a client’s Slack channel, emailing a customer, or deleting records is not.

17
00:01:24.309 --> 00:01:27.253
The irreversible ones need a human in front of them.
